-**Subtopics:**
-*   **Octree-like Subdivision:** Uses $2 \times 2 \times 2$ child grids. Best for uniform density. Link to [Multi-Level Array Grid](3329-multi-level-array-grid.md) for comparison on fixed-size blocks.
-*   **Adaptive Resolution:** Varies grid size based on local complexity. Chosen when scene density varies significantly. Link to [Macrocell Grid Traversal](5207-macrocell-grid-traversal.md) for comparison on coarse/fine layering.
+**Pitfalls**
+*   Without a good indexing strategy (such as the hash table used in JW89), it is not possible to use deep levels of recursion efficiently due to the cost of locating child nodes [S1].
+*   Build cost is generally higher than general bottom-up strategies [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Build time is higher than bottom-up strategies due to the top-down recursive nature and hash table management [S1][S2, unofficial]. Traversal time is efficient for mixed-scale scenes.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The use of hash tables for internal nodes allows sparse storage, but deep recursion requires careful indexing to avoid excessive overhead [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficiently handles scenes with both large regular objects and small dense clusters [S1][S2, unofficial]. Reduces memory accesses by skipping empty subtrees.
-*   **Limitations:** High build cost. Traversal algorithm is unsuited for GPU computation due to branching and irregular memory access patterns; CPUs are more suitable [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** CPU-based ray tracing of complex scenes with varying object density.
-*   **When not to use:** GPU ray tracing or scenes requiring very fast build times.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Traversal is $O(\log N)$ in balanced cases, but depends heavily on the hash table lookup cost and recursion depth. Build time is higher than bottom-up strategies [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the hash table overhead and the number of internal nodes. The hash table allows sparse storage of empty sub-grids but adds pointer overhead [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Efficiently handles mixed-scale scenes (large regular objects + small dense objects) [S1]. Reduces memory accesses by skipping empty regions via the hash table [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** High build cost. Not well-suited for GPU computation due to branching and pointer chasing inherent in hash-table-based recursive traversal [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** CPU-based ray tracing where scene geometry has significant scale variation and build time is not the primary bottleneck.
+*   **When not to use:** GPU ray tracing (due to branching and memory access patterns) [S2, unofficial] or scenes requiring very fast build times.
 
 ## Background
-The method was proposed by Jevans and Wyvill in 1989 [JW89]. It integrates regular and adaptive spatial subdivision methods to allow efficient ray tracing of images consisting of large regularly distributed objects and small dense objects [S1][S2, unofficial]. The survey notes that this approach has a greater build cost against general bottom-up strategies [S1][S2, unofficial].
+The method was proposed by Jevans and Wyvill in 1989 [JW89]. It is described in detail in "A Survey on Exploiting Grids for Ray Tracing" [S1] and "Grids in Ray Tracing: A Comprehensive Survey" [S2, unofficial]. The approach integrates regular grid traversal with adaptive subdivision, offering a middle ground between uniform grids and octrees.
 
